    "evidence_text": "Long-term immune deficiency after allogeneic stem cell transplantation: B-cell deficiency is associated with late infections.\n\nThe cumulative incidence of late severe infections was low (14% at four years).\n\nRisk of FN depends on chemotherapy- and patient-level factors.\n\nWe sought to determine the effects of chronic comorbidities on risk of FN.   \n\n\nWe excluded those patients who received primary prophylactic granulocyte colony-stimulating factor.\n\nHistory of comorbidities and FN events were identified using electronic medical records.\n\nIncidence of cancer after immunosuppressive treatment for heart transplantation.\n\nThe most significant increase was in the incidence of lymphomas in cardiac versus renal patients.\n\nSeveral factors may account for these differences.\n\nA questionnaire survey was carried out within European Blood and Marrow Transplantation Group (EBMT) centers.\n\nDemonstration of donor cell origin included molecular analysis of chimerism in most cases.\n\nDCL type and cytogenetic alterations were independent from the original disease.\n\nThe median time between transplantation and diagnosis of DCL was 17 months (4-164).\n\nDCL is an extremely rare complication of allogeneic SCT in which treatment attempts with chemotherapy and a second SCT are justified.\n\nDonors are not at an increased risk of developing hematologic malignancies.\n\nClinical outcomes following institution of the Canadian universal leukoreduction program for red blood cell transfusions.\n\nOBJECTIVE To evaluate clinical outcomes following adoption of a national universal prestorage leukoreduction program for blood transfusions.   \n\n\nINTERVENTION Universal prestorage leukoreduction program introduced by 2 Canadian blood agencies.\n\nSecondary outcomes included rates of posttransfusion fever and antibiotic use.   \n\n\nCONCLUSION A national universal leukoreduction program is potentially associated with decreased mortality as well as decreased fever episodes and antibiotic use after red blood cell transfusion in high-risk patients.\n\nChanges in white blood cells and platelets in children with falciparum malaria: relationship to disease outcome.\n\nThe WCC was not associated with a positive blood culture.\n\nIn children with malaria, high lymphocyte and low monocyte counts were independently associated with mortality.\n\nThe mean platelet volume was also higher in children with malaria compared with other medical conditions.\n\nThis may reflect early release from the bone marrow in response to peripheral platelet destruction.\n\nImmune reconstitution after cord blood transplantation: peculiarities, clinical implications and management strategies.\n\nUCB transplantation has traditionally been associated with delayed engraftment, poor immune reconstitution and consequent increased risk of infection.\n\nThis was true for all viruses tested.\n\nIn multivariate analysis, allo-LS was the only predictor for higher mortality (P = .04).\n\nWe hypothesize that early infection of the respiratory tract with a common cold virus makes the lungs a target for alloimmunity.\n\nSerious infections after unrelated donor transplantation in 136 children: impact of stem cell source.\n\nHow the infection risks compare after umbilical cord blood (UCB) and bone marrow (BM) transplantation is not known.",
